  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/22/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_46.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Ant-Man and the Wasp are up above the city, but all is quiet, so they return to the lab.&nbsp; Jan is clearly bored with the inaction - \"Peace! Quiet!&nbsp; How <i>ghastly</i>!\"&nbsp; Hank suggests a vacation.&nbsp; Jan suggests Greece, so off they go!&nbsp; But when they try and charter a boat to take them out to one of the islands, the owners refuse, explaining that it's because of \"the Monster!\"&nbsp; When they ask for details, one of them says \"Ask George in the radio shack!\"&nbsp; And sure enough, they approach a building with a big \"RADIO\" sign outside and a guy inside sitting a little desk with lots of equipment (I suppose that's the shack - interesting choice of names and also that a guy in Greece should be named \"George\").&nbsp; He mentions that he heard the word \"Cyclops\" just before one radio signal went dead.\n\nUltimately they find no one will to venture out with them, so they'll have to sail out there themselves.&nbsp; Jan is excited about the prospect of adventure.&nbsp; They don their costumes as they approach the island, and sure enough, a huge hand reaches down and picks up the entire ship.&nbsp; They follow him onto the island and discover the missing sailors from the other ships being held prisoner.&nbsp; Meanwhile, some strange lights in the hills seem to be holding Cyclops spellbound.&nbsp; They follow, and <i>oh no</i>!&nbsp; It's space ships!&nbsp; They're testing the captive sailors' brain impulses.\n\nAnt-Man is able to adjust his helmet and find \"electronic impulses emanating from their brains\" (the aliens), so he can now understand them.&nbsp; <i>OMG</i>, once again we read \"Earth should be classified as fit to be conquered and colonized.\" (<i>Get in line, aliens</i>!)&nbsp; Cyclops turns out to be only a robot, built by the aliens to frighten ships away and investigate the planet.\n\nJan rushes to help the captives while Hank enters the mouth of the Cyclops, looking for a way to deactivate it.&nbsp; Hank is able to override the control mechanism and send the Cyclops back toward the aliens.&nbsp; They are taken aback and surmise that \"Earthlings have even <i>stronger</i> mental control than we!\"&nbsp; They've underestimated them and flee to \"escape this accursed planet!\"&nbsp; \"Earth is too strong for us to conquer!\"&nbsp; Hank has the Cyclops return all the stolen boats and then walk down into the sea and topple onto the ocean floor to rust.\n\nA few days later, back home, Jan says to Hank, \"My dear professor, do me a favor please!&nbsp; In the future, let us have <i>no more vacations</i>!&nbsp; I just can't stand the excitement!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Mostly a yawner, with yet <i>another</i> alien race, scouting earth with the intent to conquer it (<i>geez</i>).&nbsp; The story is mundane and certainly won't gain a place in the annals of Marvel's best.&nbsp; A guy named \"George\" in Greece?&nbsp; <i>Really</i>?&nbsp; And a monster robot there to capture people so they can be scanned to determine if Earth is ripe for conquest?&nbsp; The invasion story trope is getting tiresome...(and so is this magazine for the most part).\n\nCurious that Hank calls Wasp \"Honey\" right on the cover (and yet never is there a single moment in the story where there's a hint that the two are more than partners)!&nbsp; After all that talk a couple of issues back about <i>not</i> wanting to get involved with her - he even calls her \"young lady\" somewhere in there I believe!&nbsp; Did I miss something?\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nFIXME - write something",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}